In article <1992Jun26.062407.22466@leland.Stanford.EDU>, toddc@leland.Stanford.EDU (Todd C. Merrill) writes:
> 	OK, how do I get into debug mode?  I know that doing a
> 'nethack -D' gets me into Wizard mode,

     Wizard mode is debug mode; they're two different names for the exact
same thing.

In article <1992Jun26.093114.1@vxdesy.desy.de> pawlak@vxdesy.desy.de writes:
> Sorry, nethack -D gets you into discovery mode.

     This is not correct.  Getting into wizard mode depends on the platform
you're using, but -D (for Debug) is the proper method.  If you are not
allowed to use debug mode, nethack will fall back to explore mode (which
is normally extered via -X, and is just another name for discovery mode).

In article <1992Jun26.202056.9816@leland.Stanford.EDU>, toddc@leland.Stanford.EDU (Todd C. Merrill) writes...
>	OK, then I was misled by the FAQ.  If 'nethack -D' gets me into
> discovery mode, then how _do_ I get into Wizard mode?

     A specific "wizard name" is compiled into the program.  On multi-user
systems, you must be running nethack from an account/username/whatever
which matches that name, and use -D; specifying -u some_name doesn't make
any difference in that case.  (Under VMS, the "-D" must be exclosed in
quotes.)  On single user systems (which generally don't have accounts), you
need both -D and -u some_name where 'some_name' must be the value compiled
into the program.  (Details are somewhat different for platforms that don't
have any command line available, like the Mac.)  That 'some_name' is often
"wizard" or "Wizard", but has also been known to be strange things like
"izchak"; it depends entirely on the value in place at the time the program
was compiled.
